{
  "metaTitle": "Debt Payoff Calculator: Snowball vs Avalanche",
  "metaDescription": "Free debt payoff calculator comparing debt snowball and avalanche strategies. Enter balances, APRs, and extra payments to see dates, interest, and payoff order.",
  "h1": "Debt Payoff Calculator: Snowball vs Avalanche",
  "introText": "You can clear all three sample debts totaling $19,500 in 34 months and pay $4,783.63 in total interest using the avalanche method, compared to 91 months and $12,291.39 in interest when paying only minimums. At The Modern Wallet, we built this tool so you can see your exact payoff timeline across three strategies before spending an extra dollar.\n\nTo decide which debt to pay first, make minimum payments across every balance, send your extra monthly budget to your target debt, and roll each freed minimum payment into the next balance once an account hits zero. The avalanche strategy targets the balance carrying the highest annual percentage rate (APR), while the snowball method targets the smallest balance first to build early momentum.\n\nThis calculator starts preloaded with a store card, a credit card, and a personal loan so you can immediately see the three-plan comparison. Replace the rows with your own balances, interest rates, minimum payments, and extra monthly budget to compare your payoff dates.",
  "howItWorksHeading": "Snowball vs Avalanche on Your Numbers",
  "howItWorks": "In our internal modeling, we compared avalanche, snowball, and minimum-payment schedules across $19,500 in consumer debt with a $200 extra monthly payment to measure the gap in total interest and completion time. The avalanche strategy clears all three balances in 34 months with $4,783.63 in total interest. The snowball strategy clears the debts in 35 months with $5,120.19 in interest, costing $336.56 more. Paying only minimums stretches the repayment period to 91 months and accumulates $12,291.39 in interest, which is $7,507.76 more interest and 57 additional months compared to avalanche.\n\nThe [Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB)](https://www.consumerfinance.gov/about-us/blog/how-reduce-your-debt) describes the highest interest rate method as focusing extra payments on your costliest balances to reduce total borrowing costs over the long run. In contrast, the CFPB defines the snowball method as paying off your smallest balance first so you eliminate individual obligations sooner. In our preloaded example, the snowball method eliminates the $2,500 store card in month 10, whereas the avalanche method eliminates its first balance (the $6,000 credit card at 27.99% APR) in month 22. Snowball delivers a fully cleared account 12 months earlier, which provides psychological relief at the expense of $336.56 in additional interest.\n\nThe financial difference between avalanche and snowball depends entirely on your rate spread. When all your debts share the same interest rate, the choice disappears. In our modeling of two debts at an identical 18% APR ($3,000 and $7,000 with a $150 extra payment), both avalanche and snowball finish in exactly 30 months and cost identical interest of $2,464.56. When your smallest balance also happens to carry your highest APR, the two methods follow the exact same payoff order. Modeling a $1,500 balance at 26% APR alongside a $9,000 loan at 9% APR with $100 extra per month produces an identical 35-month timeline, $1,611.43 in total interest, and a month-12 payoff for the first account across both strategies.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "How the Calculator Works",
      "body": "The calculator runs a deterministic month-by-month mathematical simulation to project your payoff dates across all open accounts. Each month, the engine adds interest to every active account by multiplying the current balance by its APR divided by 12. Next, it subtracts the required minimum payment from each account, capping that payment at the remaining balance. Finally, the tool sums your original minimum payments, your extra monthly payment, and any month-one lump sum into a unified monthly budget, directing every unallocated dollar to your target debt before spilling excess funds down the order.\n\nRolling payments forward is the core mechanism that speeds up both strategies. When you fully pay off an account, that account's required minimum payment does not disappear from your budget. Instead, the engine automatically rolls that freed payment into the extra payment pool for the next debt in line. If a lender sets a minimum payment that falls below the account's monthly accrued interest, the calculator flags that negative amortization, warning you that the balance will grow over time without higher monthly payments. For an individual account payoff or detailed amortization steps, use our [credit card payoff calculator](/credit-card-payoff/) to evaluate single-card minimums.\n\nThe model assumes fixed interest rates, on-time payments, no new charges or penalties, and consistent monthly funding throughout the payoff timeline. If you are deciding between paying accounts individually or replacing them with a single personal loan, our [debt consolidation calculator](/debt-consolidation-calculator/) evaluates taking out one new loan rather than ordering payments across existing accounts. You can also explore our detailed breakdown on [debt snowball vs avalanche](/compare/debt-snowball-vs-avalanche/) to see how psychological and mathematical factors influence long-term debt elimination."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Worked Example: Three Debts and $200 Extra",
      "body": "To see the math in action, examine the baseline scenario preloaded into the calculator. This profile includes three common consumer obligations totaling $19,500 in principal and $515 in base minimum payments. Adding $200 per month raises the total monthly debt budget to $715.\n\n| Debt Name | Starting Balance | APR | Minimum Monthly Payment |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Store card | $2,500 | 19.99% | $75 |\n| Credit card | $6,000 | 27.99% | $160 |\n| Personal loan | $11,000 | 11.00% | $280 |\n\nHere is how the three strategies perform when handling these balances:\n\n| Payoff Strategy | Debt-Free Timeline | Total Interest Paid | Total Amount Paid | Account Payoff Order |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Avalanche | 34 months | $4,783.63 | $24,283.63 | Credit card (mo. 22), Store card (mo. 26), Personal loan (mo. 34) |\n| Snowball | 35 months | $5,120.19 | $24,620.19 | Store card (mo. 10), Credit card (mo. 27), Personal loan (mo. 35) |\n| Minimums Only | 91 months | $12,291.39 | $31,791.39 | Personal loan (mo. 49), Credit card (mo. 50), Store card (mo. 91) |\n\nAvalanche minimizes total interest charges whenever interest rates differ across accounts, while snowball costs slightly more in exchange for faster early eliminations. With equal interest rates or when your smallest balance also carries your highest rate, the choice between the two methods produces identical costs. This payoff ordering applies to unsecured debts and installment loans that you can prepay without penalty. Our recommendation would change if an account carries a prepayment penalty, if you have access to a 0% promotional rate, or if you hold only a single account.\n\nThis multi-debt calculator is not intended for someone carrying only one balance, who should use the credit card payoff calculator instead. It is also not suitable for debts with active 0% promotional periods that should not be prioritized ahead of high-interest accounts. Review our guide on [how to pay off debt](/guides/how-to-pay-off-debt/) to build a practical monthly budget before accelerating your payments."
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "Which debt should I pay off first?",
      "answer": "Pay off the debt with the highest APR first if you want to pay the least total interest, which is the avalanche method. Choose your smallest balance first if you need an early milestone to stay motivated, which is the snowball method. Regardless of strategy, always make minimum payments on all other accounts."
    },
    {
      "question": "How long will it take to pay off all my debts?",
      "answer": "Payoff time depends on your total balances, interest rates, and extra payments. In our baseline $19,500 example, paying only minimums takes 91 months. Adding $200 extra per month cuts that timeline to 34 months under avalanche and 35 months under snowball, saving nearly five years of payments."
    },
    {
      "question": "Does adding a lump sum payment speed up debt payoff?",
      "answer": "Applying a lump sum immediately reduces principal, lowering monthly interest accrual across all remaining months. In our model, adding a $2,000 month-one lump sum to the sample debts reduces avalanche repayment from 34 to 30 months and drops total interest from $4,783.63 to $3,323.03, saving $1,460.60 in interest."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I use this calculator for biweekly payments?",
      "answer": "The calculator runs on monthly cycles, but you can approximate biweekly payments by adjusting your extra monthly input. Because 26 biweekly half-payments equal 13 full payments per year, divide your total monthly debt payment by 12 and enter that figure as your extra monthly payment to simulate the extra annual payment."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I include a car loan, student loan, or HELOC?",
      "answer": "Yes, you can include any debt that has a fixed balance, an APR, and a monthly minimum payment. Keep in mind that car loans and home equity lines of credit are secured by collateral, and some installment loans carry prepayment penalties that alter the financial advantage of accelerating payoff."
    },
    {
      "question": "Why might my lender's payoff date differ from this calculator?",
      "answer": "Lenders calculate interest daily based on exact billing cycles, while this calculator compounds interest monthly using APR divided by 12. Differences also occur if your APR is variable, if the lender charges account maintenance fees, or if your actual payment dates vary from month to month."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I track debt payoff in Excel or Google Sheets?",
      "answer": "You can replicate this logic in a spreadsheet by creating a row for each month and tracking interest, minimums, extra payments, and rolling balances across columns. This calculator runs those monthly allocation loops automatically, showing instant comparison totals without requiring manual formula maintenance or schedule rebuilding."
    },
    {
      "question": "Should I pay off debt or invest extra cash?",
      "answer": "Compare your guaranteed debt interest rate against your expected after-tax investment return. Paying off high-interest credit cards at 20% or higher delivers an immediate, risk-free return that beats historical market averages. Read our guide on whether to [pay off debt or invest](/guides/pay-off-debt-or-invest/) to evaluate your options."
    }
